First time an image loads (Cache Miss)
User scrolls to image
        ↓
Check LRU Cache (key = image URL)
        ↓
Not found → Cache MISS (grey dot)
        ↓
Download Bitmap from Unsplash API via HttpURLConnection
        ↓
Store Bitmap in LRU Cache (key = image URL)
        ↓
Display image in ImageView

Second time same image appears (Cache Hit)
User scrolls back up / revisits same image
        ↓
Check LRU Cache (key = image URL)
        ↓
Found → Cache HIT (green dot)
        ↓
Return Bitmap instantly from memory
        ↓
Display image in ImageView (loads in ~2ms)

Which cache is used
Only one cache — your custom LRUCache<String, Bitmap> living in memory (RAM). The key is the image URL string, the value is the decoded Bitmap object.
kotlinval cache = LRUCache<String, Bitmap>(capacity = 20)

// on load
val cached = cache.get(imageUrl)
if (cached != null) {
    imageView.setImageBitmap(cached) // hit
} else {
    val bitmap = downloadFromNetwork(imageUrl) // miss
    cache.put(imageUrl, bitmap)
    imageView.setImageBitmap(bitmap)
}

What happens when cache is full
When the 21st image is loaded:

LRU cache evicts the least recently used bitmap from memory
That image URL is removed from the map
New bitmap takes its place
If the user scrolls back to the evicted image — it's a cache miss again, re-downloaded from network


Important limitation to be aware of
Since it's in-memory only, the cache is cleared when:

App goes to background and system kills it
User force closes the app
Device runs low on memory

A production app would add a second layer — a disk cache using files — so images persist between sessions. But for this project, in-memory LRU is exactly the right scope and is what interviewers want to see.

This two-step check (memory first → network fallback) is called a cache-aside pattern and is the most common caching strategy in Android development. Want me to start writing the Kotlin implementation?You said: How can a recruiter distinguish that this is human written code rather that AIHow can a recruiter distinguish that this is human written code rather that AIMay 14Claude responded: Honest answer — they can't tell just by looking at the code.Honest answer — they can't tell just by looking at the code. But that's not actually what recruiters test for. Here's what they really do:

What recruiters actually check
1. Can you explain every line
They will ask you in the interview:

"Why did you use a doubly linked list here instead of singly linked?"
"What happens if two threads call get() simultaneously?"
"Why is your cache key a String and not an Integer?"

If you can't answer, it's obvious you didn't write it — regardless of whether AI was involved.

2. Git commit history
A recruiter or hiring manager who looks at your GitHub will check:

Did you commit incrementally or push everything in one shot?
Do your commit messages make sense? (fix null pointer in deleteNode vs add code)
Is there a progression of work visible?

This is where you actually prove the work is yours. Your LRU cache debugging session today — those are real incremental fixes you should be committing one by one.

3. Code style inconsistencies
AI tends to write very uniform, overly clean code. Real developer code has:

Your personal naming conventions
Comments where YOU were confused
A TODO or two
Small imperfections that reflect real thinking


